 | `grants` | Granted capability names; `[]`. Every requested capability must be granted before any extension setup runs. |
 | `builtinOverrides` | Optional `{ console: "extension-name" }` authorizes that registered extension to replace only the builtin console. It must declare `console` and export a host object created in the realm. No overrides by default. |
 | `limits` | Positive integer caps: `extensions: 32`, `hostObjects: 1024`, `callbacks: 1024`, `guestReferences: 1024`, `cleanups: 1024`, `nestedEvaluations: 16`. Collection budgets also apply. |
+| `callbackScheduling` | Optional `"after-prefix"` for a trusted host scheduler. Omit to retain exclusive evaluation while external callbacks are pending. |
 
 Ordinary host arguments/results are still copied. To preserve live native identity, explicitly create a host object. A guest function crossing to the host becomes an opaque callback: invoke it with `realm.invokeCallback(callback, { thisValue?, args? })`, then `realm.releaseCallback(callback)` when no longer needed. Callbacks and live objects cannot cross realms or survive close. For deferred arguments that must preserve guest identity, opt into retained references as described below.
 
 Need synchronous effects without waiting for an async callback's tail? Use `realm.startCallback(callback, options)` or `context.startCallback(callback, options)`. The frozen `CallbackInvocation` exposes two promises: await `synchronous` when the guest function returns or its async body reaches its first `await`; await `result` for the final value. Interpreter implementation awaits and budget work do not complete the prefix. Ordinary throws reject both promises; nonfatal async-function errors reject only `result`, even before the first `await`. Close, abort and fatal errors reject still-pending handles without changing a completed prefix. The same callback limits, identity and reentry rules apply; no extra grant is required. Calls started outside a host operation are queued in invocation order. Browser event/default-action policy remains the host's responsibility.
 
+With `createRealm({ callbackScheduling: "after-prefix" })`, an external host can
+await a callback's real `synchronous` handle and then call `evaluate` while its
+`result` remains pending. All admitted callbacks must have completed their first
+prefix. Source calls still reject with `code: "reentry"` while another source
+is active, including top-level await, or from a guest-owned host operation.
+Source and callback jobs share the existing realm queue, declarations, references
+and cumulative budget. The host must retain and observe callback results; later
+source may supply the state needed to settle them. No source is queued on behalf
+of a rejected call, and the option is not a guest capability or a nested-source
+grant. `invokeCallback` uses the same internal prefix boundary.
+
+Module results remain namespaces, and imports initiated by the admitted source
+remain part of its completion; independent callback imports are not joined into
+that source. Pending callbacks conservatively defer allocation reconciliation
+and compiled-ticket disposal, so more of the data budget may remain charged until
+callbacks and the source finish. Limits are not reset or enlarged.
+
+In this mode a callback failure aborts the realm and rejects its result without
+waiting for global cleanup (a joined callback must not wait on its own caller).
+Use an external `close()` to await complete cleanup, including cleanup failures.
+Calling `close()` from a guest-owned host operation initiates cancellation but
+rejects with `code: "reentry"`; awaiting cleanup there would deadlock its caller.
+External close waits for canceled source, callback and interpreter jobs to unwind
+before releasing retained resources. This scheduling option is a source proposal;
+its authored tests have not been executed or qualified for browser use.
